1
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:04,350
Out here, the night has a glow to it.

2
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:09,569
Gas with nowhere to go… burning in the dark.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:25,584
The answer was born nearly six thousand miles away — in Florence, at the Pignone foundry, in 1842.

4
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:37,510
It became Nuovo Pignone. It mastered compressors, then gas turbines — first under license from GE, then as part of GE.

5
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:39,860
Today, it is Baker Hughes.

6
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:47,060
In 2013, its engineers began a new turbine, built on decades of proven design.

7
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,356
They called it NovaLT.

8
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:53,603
Air. Twelve stages, squeezed nineteen to one.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,156
Fire. One ring of flame, fed by thirty-nine premixers, holding NOx under fifteen parts per million.

10
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:06,117
Its combustor has been tested on up to one hundred percent hydrogen.

11
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:10,486
Motion. Seven thousand eight hundred revolutions a minute.

12
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:16,301
Power. Sixteen point nine megawatts, and thirty-five thousand hours between maintenance.

13
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:21,550
The first went to work on a Canadian pipeline in 2017.

14
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:27,000
Now they're ordered by the dozen — for data centers, and for the oilfield.

15
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:37,173
Back in West Texas, the Permian produces nearly twenty-eight billion cubic feet of gas a day — more than its pipelines can carry.

16
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:41,775
This year, gas at Waha traded below zero for ninety days straight.

17
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:50,957
And yet, the field is short of power. Demand could reach twenty-six gigawatts by 2038 — and new lines are years away.

18
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:56,609
Too much gas. Not enough power.

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,862
So what if the gas never had to leave?

20
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:08,345
The fuel the field can't sell, becoming the power it can't get — made right on site, behind the meter.

21
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:13,935
That's the opportunity for Baker Hughes: gas-to-power, as a service.

22
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:23,038
NovaLT turbines, running on field gas at the pad — owned, operated, and watched around the clock by Baker Hughes.

23
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:28,390
The operator doesn't buy a power plant. They buy power, by the megawatt-hour.

24
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:34,245
Seventeen megawatts at a time. Two can run an electric frac fleet.

25
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:38,824
Moved when the field moves. Added as it grows.

26
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,342
The same fire that lit the night…

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,881
finally put to work.
